<?xml version='1.0' encoding='utf-8'?>
<chapter xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude" xml:id="infrastructure-github-overview">
  <title>GitHub: Overview</title>
  <introduction xml:id="infrastructure-github-overview-introduction">
    <p>
        GitHub is one of many web based services for managing Open Source and 
        Closed Source code bases. These services typically have a place for 
        your code (your git repository), many touch points for collaboration
        via Issues, Pull Requests, and a Wiki, as well as automation tools
        often called continuous integration. One of the automation tools
        that github provides is GitHub Pages, which allows you to have 
        a web site where you can openly publish your work just the how 
        you want to. While this chapter will get you started with 
        GitHub, there is additional
        <url href="https://docs.github.com/en/enterprise-cloud@latest/get-started">GitHub Documentation</url>
        online.
    </p>
    <p>
        GitHub was chosen for this book because it has extra support for
        schools, instructors, and active students with a .edu email account.
        This is through the <url href="https://github.com/education">GitHub Education</url> 
        community branch of GitHub.
    </p>
    </introduction>
